refsw: the reference software renderer, as an oracle for the renderers
=====================================================================

What it is
----------
refsw is skmp's reference software renderer of the Dreamcast's PowerVR2
(CLX2) CORE, from libswirl (github.com/skmp/reicast-emulator, branch alpha,
libswirl/rend/soft). It models the chip at the level of its functional
units: 32x32 tiles; the ISP rasterizing each triangle with half-edge
functions at the pixel's sampling position and interpolating 1/w (the
vertex z the game sends) with a plane equation in screen space, in 32-bit
float, into a depth buffer and a tag buffer; the depth compare modes; the
modifier volumes flipping a stencil bit where their faces pass the depth
test and summarizing it per volume; the TSP shading each pixel's tag after
the list (texture fetch with the hardware's clamp/flip/wrap and bilinear
filter, the colour combiner's four shading instructions, offset colour,
colour clamp, the fog look-up table and per-vertex fog, the blending unit
with its eight factors and the two accumulation buffers); punch-through
with the alpha test fed back into the depth write; and translucency as the
chip's layer peeling: a pass a layer from the back, equal depths ordered
by tag (submission order), which is the chip's per-pixel sort.

It is a straight, slow, readable model, not a fast renderer: a frame
takes a second or two. That is the point - it says what the chip draws at
a pixel, and why, so the renderers can be held against it.

Licence: libswirl is BSD 3-clause (license/bsd, which the files include as
their header; the full text is there). Compatible with this core's GPL.

Files
-----
From libswirl, kept as they are except where "Changes" says:
  license/bsd          the licence header the files include
  refrend_regs.h       the chip's region array / object list words
  refrend_base.h       the renderer's interface (RefRendInterface) and
                       parameter structures
  refsw.h              tile buffer layout, plane-equation interpolators,
                       the pixel pipeline's interface
  refsw.cpp            tile buffer operations, layer peeling buffers,
                       the triangle rasterizer (ISP)
  refsw_pixel.cpp      the pixel operations: depth test per render mode,
                       texture fetch, colour combiner, fog, blending
  refsw_pixel.h, refsw_tsp_init.inl  the pipeline's function tables

Written here:
  refsw_port.h         what the files above took from the rest of libswirl,
                       given from this core's headers (see its comment)
  refsw_frame.cpp      the frame walk and the checker program
  refsw_dump.h         the frame file tools/rend/tile_dump.cpp writes
  ../refsw_test.sh     builds everything, runs a game to a frame, draws it

libswirl's refrend_base.cpp - the front end that walked the chip's region
array and object lists in video memory and drove the backend tile by tile
- is not used: this core's TA parse keeps the polygons in its own lists
and writes no object lists, so refsw_frame.cpp walks those lists instead,
doing for each tile exactly the sequence refrend_base.cpp's RenderPVR()
did for a region array entry (background tag, clear, opaque list,
punch-through list, opaque modifier volumes, shade the tags, then the
translucent list peeled a layer a pass with the translucent modifier
volumes, until a pass draws nothing). Its decode of the background polygon
from video memory is carried over as it was.

Changes to libswirl's files (all of them)
-----------------------------------------
refsw.h
  - #include "rend/gles/gles.h" replaced by #include "refsw_port.h", moved
    above the other includes (it provides "using namespace std", the
    register unions and text_info the files expect)
  - Vertex::spc renamed to this core's Vertex::vtx_spc (6 places in
    IPs3::Setup)
refsw.cpp
  - the registration with libswirl's frontend at the end (#if FEAT_TA ==
    TA_LLE ... #endif: rend_refsw, rend_refsw_debug, RegisterRendererBackend)
    replaced by a plain factory, refsw_create(vram), that makes the
    refsw_impl the same way (placement new in 32-byte aligned memory)
  - (cMutex texture_lock stays; refsw_port.h gives it as a do-nothing class:
    one thread draws here)
refrend_base.h
  - DrawParameters gets a field: u32 shadow, the object's shadow bit
refsw_pixel.cpp
  - PixelFlush_tsp(): the stencil handed to InterpolateBase/InterpolateOffs
    (which scale the colour by FPU_SHAD_SCALE where bit 0 is set) is the
    pixel's stencil only when entry->params.shadow is set, else 0. libswirl
    applied the modifier volumes' result to every object; the chip applies
    it only to objects whose parameter control word has the shadow bit
    (PCW bit 7), which the object list entry carries. Without this the
    character on Jet Grind Radio's select screen, whose polygons do not
    take shadows, is drawn half dark inside its own shadow volume.
  - AlphaTest_tsp() (a punch-through polygon's TSP run from the ISP, for
    the alpha test) takes the entry of tag (tag >> 1) - 1, as
    PixelFlush_tsp() in refsw.cpp does. libswirl took tag - 1, but
    AddFpuEntry() makes a tag the entry's number times two (bit 0 is
    TAG_INVALID): every punch-through pixel was shaded with another
    entry's parameters, or with what lay past the end of the list - on Jet
    Grind Radio's street (its trees) a null tsp function, and a crash.
  - An alpha of 255 stays 255, as the chip's must: a game sets
    PT_ALPHA_REF to 255 and its punch-through texels pass where they are
    opaque (Jet Grind Radio's trees). libswirl lost the lowest bit in
    three places, so that nothing passed: the interpolated base colour is
    taken to the nearest of 0..255 (InterpolateBase(), Unorm8(); libswirl
    cut a float of 254.99 to 254), the bilinear filter's weights are 256
    - f and f (libswirl's 255 - f and f, summed in four parts each cut
    down, made 255 at most 253), and the modulating shading instructions
    multiply by base + 1 over 256 (libswirl's base over 256 made 255 x 255
    254).
Everything else - the rasterizer, the depth tests, the peeling, the texture
fetch, the colour combiner, fog, blending - is libswirl's text unchanged
(diff the files against libswirl/rend/soft to see).

What the walk takes from the parse, and the known departures
------------------------------------------------------------
- Polygons come from the parse's lists as index strips (make_index()
  joins the TA's strips of one state with repeated indices, keeping each
  triangle's parity): the triangles with a repeated index are the joins and
  are left out; the i-th triangle of a strip is rasterized with vertex_offset
  i & 1, as the chip culls by winding.
- The ISP word's texture/offset/gouraud/16-bit-UV bits are taken from the
  control word, as the TA writes them into the object's ISP word.
- A triangle's tag is its ordinal in the frame, so that a triangle gets the
  same tag each time the translucent list is walked (the peeling's
  coplanar tie-break orders equal depths by tag, lower tag first).
- Modifier volumes: each ModifierVolumeParam's triangles are rasterized in
  RM_MODIFIER with its ISP word; the volume instruction (bits 31:29, which
  refsw reads as isp.modvol.VolumeMode) summarizes the stencil after each
  triangle that carries it, as refrend_base.cpp did.
- A polygon sent under a user tile clip is drawn only in the tiles the clip
  allows, as the TA bins it (tile_clipped()).
- Textures are made by this core's texture cache (TexCache.o linked in),
  so the texels are the ones the renderers get, laid out as libswirl's
  software renderers want them (16 bytes a texel: the 2x2 block at
  (u+1,v+1), (u,v+1), (u+1,v), (u,v) in BGRA, wrapping at the edges).
  Largest level only: refsw has no mipmapping (nor trilinear, which it
  refuses). Palettes come from the dumped registers (palette_update()).
- Sprites are the two triangles the parse makes of them, not the chip's
  quad with one plane.
- The picture is the tile buffers' 8-bit BGRA; the chip's write-out to a
  16-bit 565 framebuffer (and dithering) is not applied. The pixel clip
  (FB_X_CLIP/FB_Y_CLIP) is.
- The translucent list is always peeled (autosort); refsw does not model
  the pre-sorted mode (ISP_FEED_CFG bit 0 / the region array's pre-sort
  flag), nor a translucent polygon's own depth compare mode (it forces
  less-or-equal for peeling and greater-or-equal for punch-through and
  modifier volumes, as the comments in PixelFlush_isp say).
- Render passes (several region array entries for a tile): walked in
  order; the first clears the buffers, a later one when its RenderPass
  says z_clear.

Using it
--------
  sh tools/rend/refsw_test.sh -b                        build only
  HEADLESS_DIR=sys HEADLESS_UNDUMP=20:game.raw REFSW_FROM=7 \
     sh tools/rend/refsw_test.sh game.gdi 40 reicast_use_real_bios=disabled
     a game run 40 frames with the state loaded before frame 20, the 7th
     render after that dumped and drawn (REFSW_MAX more of them, REFSW_EVERY
     apart). The state is the core's own (a RetroArch .state is that, or a
     RASTATE container whose "MEM " block is it).
  sh tools/rend/refsw_test.sh -f frame.bin 175,340 132,270
     a frame drawn again, with two pixels probed: every triangle covering
     the pixel is printed as it is rasterized with the 1/w refsw
     interpolates for it (and its bits), its depth mode, blend and texture
     words and its three vertex depths; and the depth, tag and stencil the
     tile buffers hold after each list and each peeled layer.
The frames are 9 MB each (video memory is in them). WORK=dir keeps them.
